{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1306 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c8e21996b62450e1b2e07e1ba07e7eca97b0392bee2f553a6ed170d2d08937b4" :edb/db-before-t 1305 :edb/db-after-t 1306 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534618 50 #inst "2026-10-12T04:54:05.128Z" 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186064219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186428006 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186064220 1038 #edb/ref 17592186428007 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186064411 1050 1080.0 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065076 1061 1666 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065076 1061 641164 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065077 1061 1667 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065077 1061 641165 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065078 1061 1670 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065078 1061 641168 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065079 1061 1673 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065079 1061 641174 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065124 1061 18406 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065124 1061 641171 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065182 1061 29776 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065182 1061 641177 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065381 1064 "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065381 1064 "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186065433 1064 "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186065433 1064 "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186377539 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427994 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186377539 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377540 13194139534618 17592186045417 false] [17592186377539 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427994 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186427993 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186427993 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186427993 1083 #uuid "15391250-e023-5a04-9057-9658fe4de707" 13194139534618 17592186045417 true] [17592186427994 1080 